1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,132
Here's a fresh virtual machine in the cloud:

2
00:00:03,132 --> 00:00:06,763
no copy of our code,
no virtual environment, no uv.

3
00:00:07,083 --> 00:00:09,835
It has only Docker, and we type one command.

4
00:00:10,155 --> 00:00:12,629
Just watch;
you'll run it yourself at the end.

5
00:00:12,867 --> 00:00:17,432
Docker downloads an image, in seven layers,
and starts a shell inside it.

6
00:00:17,752 --> 00:00:22,701
There we run the app, and its default text,
I love cheese, comes back in Italian.

7
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:26,619
One extra check, not in the README:
which Python is this?

8
00:00:26,939 --> 00:00:28,902
It's 3.14.7.

9
00:00:29,222 --> 00:00:33,190
Yet the recipe for this image
starts from Python 3.12.

10
00:00:33,510 --> 00:00:36,113
Hold that thought;
we'll solve it twice today.

11
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:39,614
That's where today's tutorial,
simple-translate, ends.

12
00:00:39,934 --> 00:00:45,865
Last lecture we climbed its first two rungs:
a manual install on a VM, then uv.

13
00:00:46,185 --> 00:00:47,677
Today, the other two:

14
00:00:47,677 --> 00:00:51,158
package the app
as a Docker image on your laptop,

15
00:00:51,158 --> 00:00:54,099
push it to Docker Hub, and run it on a VM.

16
00:00:54,333 --> 00:00:57,576
To follow along,
open the README beside this video.

17
00:00:57,896 --> 00:01:03,528
Three trackers, top left, show where we are:
on the laptop, on Docker Hub, on the VM.

18
00:01:03,848 --> 00:01:08,077
When the yellow your-turn tag appears,
pause and run the command;

19
00:01:08,077 --> 00:01:11,720
a gray extra tag marks a check
that isn't in the README.

20
00:01:11,967 --> 00:01:15,777
You need Docker Desktop running,
a free Docker Hub account,

21
00:01:15,777 --> 00:01:19,135
and your Google Cloud project
for the VM at the end.

22
00:01:19,455 --> 00:01:21,652
No service account or keys today.

23
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:24,983
First, four words for the whole lecture.

24
00:01:25,303 --> 00:01:28,817
In lecture 1,
every new VM needed the same setup,

25
00:01:28,817 --> 00:01:32,044
on an operating system
that had to cooperate.

26
00:01:32,364 --> 00:01:36,212
A Docker image packs it all
into one thing you can copy:

27
00:01:36,212 --> 00:01:39,511
system files, Python,
the packages and the code.

28
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:42,459
An image is a stack of read-only layers.

29
00:01:42,779 --> 00:01:46,961
Each layer holds the files added
or changed by one build step.

30
00:01:47,281 --> 00:01:49,581
Beside it sits a small config card:

31
00:01:49,581 --> 00:01:53,787
settings like environment variables,
and which program to start.

32
00:01:54,033 --> 00:01:57,830
The recipe is a Dockerfile,
read one instruction at a time.

33
00:01:58,150 --> 00:02:02,161
Instructions that change files,
like RUN and COPY, add a layer.

34
00:02:02,481 --> 00:02:05,425
Others, like CMD, only write on the card.

35
00:02:05,667 --> 00:02:09,328
Run an image and you get a container:
the same layers,

36
00:02:09,328 --> 00:02:13,667
shared, plus a thin writable layer on top,
and one main process.

37
00:02:13,987 --> 00:02:17,315
When that process ends, the container stops,

38
00:02:17,315 --> 00:02:20,643
and --rm deletes it, writable layer and all.

39
00:02:20,963 --> 00:02:22,755
The image never changes.

40
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,883
Think of the image as a class,
and each container as an instance.

41
00:02:27,203 --> 00:02:31,171
One small slip in the README:
it says build the docker container.

42
00:02:31,491 --> 00:02:34,563
Building makes an image;
running makes a container.

43
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:39,173
A registry, like Docker Hub,
stores images by name, on shelves.

44
00:02:39,493 --> 00:02:42,971
A tag is a name that points at one image,
such as latest.

45
00:02:43,217 --> 00:02:45,670
And a container is not a small VM.

46
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:50,065
A VM boots its own kernel,
the core of the operating system.

47
00:02:50,385 --> 00:02:54,587
Containers are isolated processes
that share one Linux kernel.

48
00:02:54,983 --> 00:02:56,822
On a Mac or Windows laptop,

49
00:02:56,822 --> 00:03:01,319
Docker Desktop provides that kernel
with a small, hidden Linux VM.

50
00:03:01,639 --> 00:03:05,671
On a cloud VM,
Docker runs right on the VM's own Linux.

51
00:03:05,917 --> 00:03:08,833
What a venv couldn't isolate in lecture 1,

52
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:12,722
the system's files and libraries,
is now inside the box.

53
00:03:13,042 --> 00:03:15,666
GPU drivers still come from the host.

54
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:20,676
Now the README section
Developing App using Containers.

55
00:03:20,996 --> 00:03:23,044
Open a terminal on your laptop.

56
00:03:23,364 --> 00:03:26,152
On a Mac your prompt looks different;

57
00:03:26,152 --> 00:03:30,297
on Windows,
use an Ubuntu terminal in WSL, or Git Bash.

58
00:03:30,617 --> 00:03:34,106
In Git Bash,
put winpty in front of docker run

59
00:03:34,106 --> 00:03:36,761
if it says stdin is not a terminal.

60
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,392
First, docker container ls
lists running containers.

61
00:03:40,712 --> 00:03:43,677
Here it shows only the header,
so nothing is running.

62
00:03:43,997 --> 00:03:48,861
If yours can't connect to the Docker daemon,
start Docker Desktop and try again.

63
00:03:49,181 --> 00:03:50,333
Pause and run it.

64
00:03:50,583 --> 00:03:52,844
Next, stop any running container.

65
00:03:53,164 --> 00:03:54,885
There's nothing to stop here;

66
00:03:54,885 --> 00:03:57,794
if you have one,
docker stop with its ID does it.

67
00:03:58,033 --> 00:03:59,676
Then docker system prune.

68
00:03:59,996 --> 00:04:02,982
It lists what it will remove and asks;
type y.

69
00:04:03,302 --> 00:04:04,861
Nothing to reclaim here;

70
00:04:04,861 --> 00:04:09,212
yours may delete the stopped hello-world
container from Tutorial 0.

71
00:04:09,532 --> 00:04:10,684
Pause and run it.

72
00:04:10,933 --> 00:04:14,944
Look at the third item:
dangling images, the ones without a name.

73
00:04:15,264 --> 00:04:18,400
Despite the README's heading,
named images survive.

74
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:22,027
With -a,
prune would remove every unused image.

75
00:04:22,267 --> 00:04:25,104
Then docker image ls lists your images.

76
00:04:25,424 --> 00:04:29,003
The list is empty here;
yours may still show hello-world:

77
00:04:29,003 --> 00:04:30,886
it has a name, so it survived.

78
00:04:31,206 --> 00:04:36,112
Your columns may differ:
this is Docker 29, at the time of recording.

79
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:37,417
Now the code.

80
00:04:37,737 --> 00:04:42,249
As the README notes,
a clone on last lecture's VM doesn't help:

81
00:04:42,249 --> 00:04:44,755
Docker Desktop runs on your laptop.

82
00:04:45,075 --> 00:04:48,019
Clone it here,
then cd into simple-translate.

83
00:04:48,339 --> 00:04:49,641
Pause and run both.

84
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:53,104
An extra check, not in the README: ls -a.

85
00:04:53,424 --> 00:04:56,447
There's the solution, Dockerfile.txt,

86
00:04:56,447 --> 00:05:00,614
but no file called just Dockerfile,
and no uv.lock.

87
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:05,799
Run the README's build command now,
as an extra: failed to read dockerfile.

88
00:05:06,119 --> 00:05:08,850
The README wants you to write it,
in five steps.

89
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:12,783
Next, inside the simple-translate folder,

90
00:05:12,783 --> 00:05:16,791
create a file called Dockerfile:
capital D, no extension.

91
00:05:17,111 --> 00:05:21,420
The README fills it in five steps,
with a build and a run after each.

92
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:23,383
Pause and create the file.

93
00:05:23,617 --> 00:05:29,356
Dockerfile step 1 is one instruction:
FROM python:3.12-slim-bookworm.

94
00:05:29,676 --> 00:05:34,177
That's the official Python image,
on a slim Debian 12 called bookworm.

95
00:05:34,497 --> 00:05:37,441
Paste it in, save,
and check with cat, an extra.

96
00:05:37,683 --> 00:05:39,048
Now the build command.

97
00:05:39,368 --> 00:05:43,976
-t names the image simple-translate;
-f picks the recipe.

98
00:05:44,296 --> 00:05:46,773
And the final dot is the build context:

99
00:05:46,773 --> 00:05:49,758
this folder,
which Docker sends to the builder.

100
00:05:50,078 --> 00:05:52,083
Step 3 shows why that matters.

101
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:53,826
Pause and run the build.

102
00:05:54,146 --> 00:05:56,216
Your terminal shows a compact view.

103
00:05:56,536 --> 00:06:01,357
Here you see the full log;
to get it, add --progress=plain.

104
00:06:01,677 --> 00:06:02,921
On Docker Desktop,

105
00:06:02,921 --> 00:06:06,307
the builder is called desktop-linux,
not default.

106
00:06:06,550 --> 00:06:11,614
Docker fetches the base image, four layers,
under 50 megabytes compressed,

107
00:06:11,614 --> 00:06:15,446
and saves the result as simple-translate,
tagged latest.

108
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:18,262
Those four layers
are the bottom of our stack.

109
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:20,100
What will it do when it starts?

110
00:06:20,420 --> 00:06:24,203
An extra docker image inspect
reads the config card:

111
00:06:24,203 --> 00:06:29,295
no entrypoint, and the command python3,
inherited from the base image.

112
00:06:29,615 --> 00:06:31,492
So we predict a Python prompt.

113
00:06:31,733 --> 00:06:37,450
Now the run command:
docker run --rm -ti simple-translate.

114
00:06:37,770 --> 00:06:42,656
--rm cleans up afterwards;
-ti lets you type into it.

115
00:06:42,976 --> 00:06:44,128
Pause and run it.

116
00:06:44,448 --> 00:06:48,864
There's the Python prompt,
3.12.15 at the time of recording.

117
00:06:49,184 --> 00:06:51,445
Type exit() to leave.

118
00:06:51,683 --> 00:06:55,389
One surprise:
an extra git status says nothing to commit,

119
00:06:55,389 --> 00:06:57,144
though we just made a file.

120
00:06:57,464 --> 00:07:00,823
The .gitignore file here
lists Dockerfile on line 3,

121
00:07:00,823 --> 00:07:02,051
so git hides yours.

122
00:07:02,371 --> 00:07:05,081
To commit it anyway, use git add -f.

123
00:07:06,117 --> 00:07:10,213
Step 2 adds one line: ENTRYPOINT /bin/bash.

124
00:07:10,533 --> 00:07:12,581
Put it under the FROM line, and save.

125
00:07:12,817 --> 00:07:14,204
Pause and build again.

126
00:07:14,524 --> 00:07:16,164
The FROM step says CACHED:

127
00:07:16,164 --> 00:07:19,004
nothing is downloaded,
and no layer is added.

128
00:07:19,324 --> 00:07:22,118
ENTRYPOINT only writes on the config card.

129
00:07:22,367 --> 00:07:27,935
Think of ENTRYPOINT as the program to start,
and CMD as its default arguments.

130
00:07:28,255 --> 00:07:32,714
The entrypoint is now bash,
and the command slot still says python3.

131
00:07:33,034 --> 00:07:36,212
So will the container start bash,
running python3?

132
00:07:36,450 --> 00:07:38,265
This extra inspect says no:

133
00:07:38,265 --> 00:07:41,762
the entrypoint is bash,
and the command is now null.

134
00:07:42,082 --> 00:07:46,541
Setting an entrypoint resets the command
you inherited from the base image.

135
00:07:46,861 --> 00:07:48,568
So bash starts on its own.

136
00:07:48,817 --> 00:07:50,886
Pause and run it: a bare prompt.

137
00:07:51,206 --> 00:07:52,593
Read it left to right.

138
00:07:52,913 --> 00:07:55,985
You're root, the administrator,
inside the container.

139
00:07:56,305 --> 00:07:59,237
After the @ sign comes the container's ID,

140
00:07:59,237 --> 00:08:02,449
which changes every run,
so yours will differ.

141
00:08:02,684 --> 00:08:06,452
And the slash means you start
in the top folder of the image,

142
00:08:06,452 --> 00:08:09,233
because this image sets
no working directory.

143
00:08:09,553 --> 00:08:10,940
Type exit to leave.

144
00:08:11,983 --> 00:08:14,031
Step 3 adds two instructions.

145
00:08:14,351 --> 00:08:19,407
RUN pip install uv uses the pip
of the base image to install uv.

146
00:08:19,727 --> 00:08:25,231
And the COPY line copies this folder, .,
into the image's top folder, ./.

147
00:08:25,551 --> 00:08:27,471
Add both at the end, and save.

148
00:08:27,716 --> 00:08:29,849
Here's why the final dot matters.

149
00:08:30,169 --> 00:08:33,071
Docker packs the whole folder
into one parcel,

150
00:08:33,071 --> 00:08:36,036
the build context,
and sends it to the builder.

151
00:08:36,356 --> 00:08:40,803
Every file goes in, the hidden git folder
and your Dockerfile too;

152
00:08:40,803 --> 00:08:44,150
a file called .dockerignore
could leave some out,

153
00:08:44,150 --> 00:08:45,721
but this repo has none.

154
00:08:46,041 --> 00:08:49,604
The COPY line unpacks it all
into the image's top folder.

155
00:08:49,850 --> 00:08:51,023
Pause and build.

156
00:08:51,343 --> 00:08:54,866
The log confirms it:
there's no .dockerignore file,

157
00:08:54,866 --> 00:08:58,319
so nothing is filtered out,
and the whole context,

158
00:08:58,319 --> 00:09:01,220
63 kilobytes here, travels to the builder.

159
00:09:01,466 --> 00:09:07,044
Then pip installs uv,
version 0.13.0 at the time of recording,

160
00:09:07,044 --> 00:09:09,743
and COPY runs: two new layers.

161
00:09:10,063 --> 00:09:12,410
Pip also warns about running as root.

162
00:09:12,730 --> 00:09:17,914
In lecture 1 that warning mattered;
here, the image itself is the isolation.

163
00:09:18,933 --> 00:09:23,306
Something to ponder: uv is installed,
and the code is in the image.

164
00:09:23,626 --> 00:09:25,802
Will python cli.py run?

165
00:09:32,650 --> 00:09:37,269
No. Pause and run it,
then look around with ls, an extra:

166
00:09:37,269 --> 00:09:40,863
our files sit right next
to the system's own folders.

167
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:48,033
Then python cli.py, another extra:
ModuleNotFoundError, No module named 'art'.

168
00:09:48,353 --> 00:09:51,169
We installed the installer,
not the packages.

169
00:09:51,489 --> 00:09:52,534
Type exit.

170
00:09:54,383 --> 00:09:58,287
Step 4 adds RUN uv sync;
add it at the end, and save.

171
00:09:58,607 --> 00:10:00,687
Like on last lecture's VM,

172
00:10:00,687 --> 00:10:05,647
it reads pyproject.toml
and installs the packages into a venv.

173
00:10:05,883 --> 00:10:06,928
Now predict.

174
00:10:07,248 --> 00:10:10,101
This image's Python is 3.12,

175
00:10:10,101 --> 00:10:15,398
and line 6 of pyproject.toml asks
for 3.13 or newer.

176
00:10:15,718 --> 00:10:17,830
What will uv sync do during the build?

177
00:10:26,666 --> 00:10:27,839
Pause and build.

178
00:10:28,159 --> 00:10:32,110
First, notice: the pip step says CACHED,
but COPY says DONE:

179
00:10:32,110 --> 00:10:35,007
it ran again, although we only added a line.

180
00:10:35,327 --> 00:10:37,312
Keep that question for part 2.

181
00:10:37,550 --> 00:10:38,723
Now the answer.

182
00:10:39,043 --> 00:10:45,258
As on the Debian 12 VM in lecture 1,
uv downloads its own Python:

183
00:10:45,258 --> 00:10:49,177
CPython 3.15.0, at the time of recording.

184
00:10:49,497 --> 00:10:54,382
Then it creates a venv at /.venv,
and installs 11 packages.

185
00:10:54,616 --> 00:10:58,648
On the number line,
the requirement covers 3.13 and newer.

186
00:10:58,968 --> 00:11:01,489
The base image's 3.12 falls outside,

187
00:11:01,489 --> 00:11:05,901
so uv downloads the newest stable
Python it knows in the range,

188
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:08,632
and the python in the venv links to it.

189
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:16,526
Pause and run it, then two extras:

190
00:11:16,526 --> 00:11:21,344
ls -a, which also lists hidden files,
and python cli.py.

191
00:11:21,664 --> 00:11:25,333
Now the listing shows .venv,
and a new uv.lock.

192
00:11:25,653 --> 00:11:30,581
But python cli.py fails exactly as before.
Why?

193
00:11:30,816 --> 00:11:34,016
Remember lecture 1: activation is just PATH.

194
00:11:34,336 --> 00:11:36,379
Nothing has activated the venv,

195
00:11:36,379 --> 00:11:41,717
so the word python first finds the Python
in /usr/local/bin, from the base image.

196
00:11:42,037 --> 00:11:45,366
That's 3.12,
and it has none of our packages.

197
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:49,792
Activating the venv
would put its bin folder in front,

198
00:11:49,792 --> 00:11:52,043
and python would find 3.15.0.

199
00:11:52,363 --> 00:11:58,140
Two extra checks confirm it:
the venv's own python reports 3.15.0,

200
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:03,392
and after sourcing the activate script,
python cli.py works.

201
00:12:03,712 --> 00:12:06,080
The prompt now shows (app).

202
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:08,661
Type exit to get back to your laptop.

203
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:13,324
So this image carries two Pythons:
3.12 from the base,

204
00:12:13,324 --> 00:12:17,092
which only installed uv,
and 3.15 for the app.

205
00:12:17,412 --> 00:12:21,487
That's half of the opening puzzle,
and a small mismatch in the tutorial.

206
00:12:21,807 --> 00:12:25,697
In your own projects,
start from a Python 3.13 image,

207
00:12:25,697 --> 00:12:28,633
or make requires-python match the image.

208
00:12:30,067 --> 00:12:33,224
Step 5 adds the last instruction, CMD.

209
00:12:33,544 --> 00:12:39,240
It holds -c and one quoted command:
source the activate script, then exec bash.

210
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:41,224
Add it at the end, and save.

211
00:12:41,467 --> 00:12:46,382
On the card, the entrypoint
and the command join into one command line:

212
00:12:46,382 --> 00:12:48,251
bash, -c, then that string.

213
00:12:48,571 --> 00:12:51,942
On its own,
bash -c runs the string and exits.

214
00:12:52,184 --> 00:12:55,258
Here the string first
sources the activate script,

215
00:12:55,258 --> 00:12:58,456
which puts the bin folder
of the venv first in PATH.

216
00:12:58,776 --> 00:13:00,580
Then exec bash swaps this bash

217
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:04,792
for an interactive one that keeps
the new PATH and gives you a prompt.

218
00:13:05,034 --> 00:13:06,207
Pause and build.

219
00:13:06,527 --> 00:13:08,496
Again COPY and uv sync run,

220
00:13:08,496 --> 00:13:11,925
and Python downloads again,
for one added line.

221
00:13:12,245 --> 00:13:13,802
Part 2 explains why.

222
00:13:14,050 --> 00:13:15,202
Pause and run it.

223
00:13:15,522 --> 00:13:19,127
No (app) this time; is the venv active?

224
00:13:19,447 --> 00:13:24,365
These extra checks say yes:
which python points into .venv,

225
00:13:24,365 --> 00:13:28,450
it's 3.15.0,
and the VIRTUAL_ENV variable is set.

226
00:13:28,770 --> 00:13:30,733
The fresh bash set its own prompt.

227
00:13:31,053 --> 00:13:37,325
The lecture's run command adds --entrypoint,
which drops this CMD, and the venv with it.

228
00:13:37,645 --> 00:13:40,056
Then type exit, back to your laptop.

229
00:13:40,300 --> 00:13:44,396
The README then points to a solution,
Dockerfile.txt.

230
00:13:44,716 --> 00:13:46,968
An extra diff shows what it adds;

231
00:13:46,968 --> 00:13:50,860
if yours prints less-than
and greater-than signs, add -u.

232
00:13:51,180 --> 00:13:53,053
First, three ENV lines:

233
00:13:53,053 --> 00:13:57,858
no questions from apt,
a UTF-8 locale, and unbuffered logs.

234
00:13:58,100 --> 00:14:02,531
Second, one bigger RUN:
update and upgrade the Debian packages,

235
00:14:02,531 --> 00:14:05,204
clean up, upgrade pip, and install uv.

236
00:14:05,524 --> 00:14:07,745
And ENTRYPOINT moved to the end:

237
00:14:07,745 --> 00:14:11,007
the order of ENTRYPOINT and CMD
doesn't matter.

238
00:14:11,250 --> 00:14:13,298
The README only names the file.

239
00:14:13,618 --> 00:14:16,791
So here's the quickest way,
an extra command:

240
00:14:16,791 --> 00:14:18,695
copy it over yours with cp.

241
00:14:19,015 --> 00:14:20,253
Pause and do that.

242
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:23,306
Now the README's Build Docker Image section:

243
00:14:23,306 --> 00:14:26,431
from the simple-translate folder,
the same build.

244
00:14:26,751 --> 00:14:27,903
Pause and run it.

245
00:14:28,223 --> 00:14:30,542
This compact view is what you'll see:

246
00:14:30,542 --> 00:14:33,364
four steps,
the apt and pip step the slowest.

247
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:41,845
Now the README's Run Docker Container step:
docker run --rm -ti simple-translate.

248
00:14:42,165 --> 00:14:44,964
At the prompt,
type the README's translation:

249
00:14:44,964 --> 00:14:47,328
Good morning, from English to Spanish.

250
00:14:47,648 --> 00:14:48,949
Pause and run both.

251
00:14:49,183 --> 00:14:51,284
Good morning comes back in Spanish:

252
00:14:51,284 --> 00:14:55,967
the request went from the container,
through your laptop's network, to Google.

253
00:14:56,287 --> 00:15:00,521
If yours ends in a runtime error,
Google refused the request:

254
00:15:00,521 --> 00:15:02,325
try later; Docker is fine.

255
00:15:02,567 --> 00:15:05,703
To leave, type exit,
and you're back on your laptop.

256
00:15:06,023 --> 00:15:07,196
Pause and do it.

257
00:15:07,433 --> 00:15:08,734
What did exit do?

258
00:15:09,054 --> 00:15:11,505
It ended the container's process,

259
00:15:11,505 --> 00:15:14,921
so the container stopped,
and --rm deleted it.

260
00:15:15,241 --> 00:15:20,468
An extra check, docker ps -a,
lists every container, even stopped ones.

261
00:15:20,788 --> 00:15:23,476
Nothing is left; the image is untouched.

262
00:15:23,717 --> 00:15:26,191
In a second run, an extra check,

263
00:15:26,191 --> 00:15:29,669
echo $$,
shows that bash is process number 1.

264
00:15:29,989 --> 00:15:33,123
That's the PID namespace
from today's lecture:

265
00:15:33,123 --> 00:15:36,325
each container counts
its own processes from 1.

266
00:15:36,645 --> 00:15:39,397
The container lives as long as that process.

267
00:15:39,717 --> 00:15:43,600
-i connects your keyboard,
and -t gives it a terminal.

268
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:45,178
Then type exit.

269
00:15:46,517 --> 00:15:47,520
Now a puzzle.

270
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:51,253
Remember,
CMD only holds the default arguments.

271
00:15:51,573 --> 00:15:55,023
What if you put words right
after the image name:

272
00:15:55,023 --> 00:15:58,613
docker run --rm -ti
simple-translate python cli.py?

273
00:15:58,933 --> 00:16:01,194
What command line does the container run?

274
00:16:10,633 --> 00:16:14,814
Words after the image name replace CMD,
and only CMD.

275
00:16:15,134 --> 00:16:18,761
So the container runs
/bin/bash python cli.py.

276
00:16:19,081 --> 00:16:21,376
Bash takes python as a script name,

277
00:16:21,376 --> 00:16:24,457
and finds the Python program
in /usr/local/bin.

278
00:16:24,777 --> 00:16:31,604
That's a binary, so bash refuses:
cannot execute binary file, exit code 126.

279
00:16:31,850 --> 00:16:36,128
To run it in one go,
replace CMD with your own -c string,

280
00:16:36,128 --> 00:16:40,106
as in this extra:
activate, then translate to French.

281
00:16:41,350 --> 00:16:46,313
A repo helper,
docker-shell.sh, builds and runs in one go:

282
00:16:46,313 --> 00:16:48,966
run it with sh docker-shell.sh.

283
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:51,227
You've built and run your own image.

284
00:16:51,547 --> 00:16:53,421
In part 2: what's inside it,

285
00:16:53,421 --> 00:16:56,901
why it keeps rebuilding,
and how to ship it to a VM.

